  "client_view_text": " A process is a program in execution, which includes the program code, data, and system resources allocated to it. A thread is a flow of execution within a process, which shares the same memory space and resources as the process. In other words, a process is a container that holds one or more threads, and each thread executes a portion of the program code.\n\nHere are some key differences between processes and threads:\n\n1.  **Process**: A process is a separate entity that runs independently of other processes. It has its own memory space, and each process has its own copy of the program code, data, and system resources.\n\n2 A process is a program in execution, which includes the program code, data, and system resources allocated to it. A thread is a flow of execution within a process, which shares the same memory space and resources as the process. In other words, a process is a container that holds one or more threads. Here are some key differences between processes and threads:\n\n1.  **Process**: A process is a separate entity that runs independently of other processes. It has its own memory space, and each process has its own copy of the program code, data, and system resources. When a process is created, a new memory space is allocated to it, and it has its own program counter, stack, and registers.\n\n2.  **Thread**: A thread is a lightweight process that shares the same memory space and resources as the process it belongs to. Threads are created within a process, and they share the same memory space, program counter, stack, and registers. Threads are useful for improving the responsiveness of an application, as they can run concurrently and handle multiple tasks simultaneously.\n\nHere are some key differences between processes and threads:\n\n*   **Memory Usage**: Processes have their own memory space, while threads share the same memory space as the process they belong to.\n*   **"
